        <section class="docs-section" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <p class="docs-eyebrow">Help</p>
            <h2 id="faq-title">FAQ</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-grid">
            <article>
              <h3>Where are recordings saved?</h3>
              <p>
                By default, recordings are saved in <code>~/Movies/Luxel</code>. Change the folder
                in Settings > Output.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Can I export audio in GIF or APNG?</h3>
              <p>
                No. GIF and APNG are animated image formats in Luxel and drop audio.
                Use MP4, HEVC MP4, ProRes MOV, WebM, or AV1 when audio is required.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>How does camera preview work?</h3>
              <p>
                Choose a camera in Recording settings or the area picker. Luxel shows a
                floating camera preview that can appear in the captured area. You can
                choose Squircle, Rounded, or Square shape; small, medium, or large size;
                and mirror behavior. Cutout removes the camera background around the
                presenter, while Green Screen removes a chroma-key background.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Can I export audio-only recordings?</h3>
              <p>
                Yes. Audio-only recordings are saved as <code>.m4a</code> with AAC or
                ALAC. The editor and CLI can export audio-only sources as M4A, ALAC M4A,
                WAV, CAF, or FLAC.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Can I control Luxel from scripts?</h3>
              <p>
                Yes. Use the <code>luxel</code> command for shell workflows, or use the{" "}
                <code>luxel://</code> URL scheme from apps that can open URLs.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Does Replay Buffer record all the time?</h3>
              <p>
                No. Replay Buffer is off until you enable it. While active, it is visible
                in the menu bar and can be stopped from the same controls.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Can I use Luxel without opening the editor?</h3>
              <p>
                Yes. Quick exports and replay clip destinations can finish directly. In Developer
                ID builds, <code>luxel convert</code>, <code>luxel export</code>, and{" "}
                <code>luxel transcribe</code> also run without opening the editor.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Which languages are localized?</h3>
              <p>
                Luxel includes English, German, Spanish, French, Italian, Japanese, Korean,
                Vietnamese, Simplified Chinese, Portuguese (Brazil), and Portuguese (Portugal).
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </main>
    <script>
      const lazyVideos = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll("video[data-lazy-src]"));

      const loadLazyVideo = (video) => {
        if (!(video instanceof HTMLVideoElement) || video.src) {
          return;
        }

        const src = video.dataset.lazySrc;
        if (!src) {
          return;
        }

        video.src = src;
        video.load();
        video.play().catch(() => {});
      };

      if ("IntersectionObserver" in window) {
        const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries) => {
            entries.forEach((entry) => {
              if (!entry.isIntersecting) {
                return;
              }

              loadLazyVideo(entry.target);
              observer.unobserve(entry.target);
            });
          },
          { rootMargin: "420px 0px" }
        );

        lazyVideos.forEach((video) => observer.observe(video));
      } else {
        lazyVideos.forEach(loadLazyVideo);
      }
    </script>
</PageLayout>

